.TH PTY_FEED 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_feed, pty_trace_enable \- feed the emulator directly; trace output
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "void pty_feed(PtySession *" s ", const char *" data ", size_t " len );
.BI "void pty_trace_enable(int " on );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pty_feed ()
processes
.I len
bytes as if the child had written them.
It works on a session with no child at all, which makes it the tool for
testing a renderer in-process: capture the bytes it produces (to a
memory stream, for instance), feed them, and inspect the screen with the
same functions and the same full-screen comparison as a PTY test.
Sequences may be split at any byte between calls.
If a child is running, answers to terminal queries in
.I data
(cursor position, device attributes) are sent to it; otherwise they are
discarded.
.PP
.BR pty_trace_enable ()
turns on (non-zero) or off the printing of every chunk read from any
child to standard output: its size and up to 120 bytes, with ESC and
non-printable bytes shown symbolically.
The setting is process-wide.
Use it while writing a test, not in a finished one.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
char *buf; size_t len;
FILE *fp = open_memstream(&buf, &len);
render_status_bar(fp, &model);          /* the code under test */
fclose(fp);

PtySession *s = pty_open(40, 1);
pty_feed(s, buf, len);
const char *const expected[] = { " INBOX  3 unread        12:00", NULL };
ASSERT_SCREEN_EQ(s, expected);
ASSERT_CELL_ATTR(s, 0, 1, PTY_ATTR_BOLD);
pty_close(s);
free(buf);
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_region_compare (3),
.BR libptytest (7)
